-- title:
--   Finiteness of the integral closure of k[x] in a function field
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field and let $F$ be a field equipped with a $k$-algebra structure. Let $x \in F$ be transcendental over $k$, that is, $x$ is not a root of any nonzero polynomial with coefficients in $k$, and assume that $F$ is finite-dimensional as a vector space over the intermediate field $\mathrm{IntermediateField.adjoin}\ k\ \{x\}$, the subfield $k(x)$ of $F$ generated by $k$ and $x$. Write $k[x]$ for the $k$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Algebra.adjoin}\ k\ \{x\}$ of $F$ generated by $x$, a polynomial ring in one variable since $x$ is transcendental. The conclusion is that the integral closure of $k[x]$ in $F$, namely the subalgebra of those elements of $F$ that satisfy a monic polynomial equation with coefficients in $k[x]$, is a finite $k[x]$-module, i.e. finitely generated as a module over $k[x]$. No separability hypothesis is imposed on $F$ over $k(x)$, and no restriction on the characteristic of $k$.
--
--   This is the finiteness of the integral closure of a polynomial ring in one variable inside a one-variable algebraic function field over an algebraically closed (hence perfect) base field, the statement that makes the ring of functions regular away from the fibre over $x=\infty$ a finite $k[x]$-module, and therefore free of rank $[F:k(x)]$. It is used in the construction of Gauss bases for regular prolongations along an algebraic curve.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Algebra_finite_integralClosure_adjoin_singleton_of_isAlgClosed.lean

